  {
   "id": "ODR-2",
   "title": "A base class does not also hold what its subtypes declare",
   "why": "The schema comments these particles out of `_OpenDriveElement` and `t_junction` \"to comply with the sequence order of earlier OpenDRIVE versions\": every subtype declares them itself, in its own place. The model draws them on the base class as well, so a UML reader sees each of them twice on every subtype. They are removed from the base class, which is what the schema implements.",
   "schema": "unchanged: the normative schema already leaves them out",
   "operations": [
    {
     "op": "remove-connector",
     "connector": 6
    },
    {
     "op": "remove-connector",
     "connector": 86
    },
    {
     "op": "remove-connector",
     "connector": 87
    },
    {
     "op": "remove-connector",
     "connector": 88
    },
    {
     "op": "remove-connector",
     "connector": 243
    },
    {
     "op": "remove-connector",
     "connector": 354
    },
    {
     "op": "remove-connector",
     "connector": 355
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ODR-3",
   "title": "An element is one association",
   "why": "`t_junction_virtual` draws its `connection` element three times, once to its declared type and once to each of the two types its `xs:alternative`s select. UML gives a class one property per name, so a reader sees three conflicting `connection` properties. The alternatives are already stated by their `XSDAlternative_*` tags; the association to the declared type is kept.",
   "schema": "unchanged: the derivation declares one element per role",
   "operations": [
    {
     "op": "remove-connector",
     "connector": 255
    },
    {
     "op": "remove-connector",
     "connector": 538
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ODR-4",
   "title": "The junction's type alternatives are numbered in the schema's order",
   "why": "`XSDAlternative_nr` orders an element's type alternatives, and the first whose test holds assigns the type. The model numbers `junction`'s crossing 1, direct 2 and virtual 3; the schema lists virtual, direct, crossing. The tests are mutually exclusive, so the language is the same, but the model should say what the schema does.",
   "schema": "unchanged",
   "operations": [
    {
     "op": "set-tag",
     "class": 181,
     "name": "XSDAlternative_nr",
     "value": "1"
    },
    {
     "op": "set-tag",
     "class": 186,
     "name": "XSDAlternative_nr",
     "value": "3"
    }
   ]
  },
